@node Overview
@chapter Overview

Twist is a transfer library and a small command-line client.  The
public interface is @code{twist_easy}, @code{twist_multi} and
@code{twist_share}.  Names, constants and header files are Twist's own.

The schemes that are implemented are listed under @ref{Protocols}.
Schemes that are not yet implemented have a row in the scheme table, so
a handler can be added without changing the transfer engine.  TLS and QUIC come from
GNU Quickstep.  The reactor does not start a thread of its own.  A
transfer's sockets stay on one loop, and QUIC is one UDP socket on
that same loop.

@node Library
@chapter Library

Call @code{twist_global_init} before any other function, and
@code{twist_global_cleanup} when the process is finished with the
library.

An easy handle is created with @code{twist_easy_init}, configured with
@code{twist_easy_setopt}, run with @code{twist_easy_perform} and
released with @code{twist_easy_cleanup}.  Several easy handles can
share one loop through @code{twist_multi_init} and
@code{twist_multi_socket_action}.  @code{twist_multi_timeout} is the
number of milliseconds until the next timer, or @minus{}1 when nothing
is scheduled.

@node Invocation
@chapter Invocation

@example
twist [OPTION]... URL...
@end example

The body is written to standard output, or to the file named by
@option{-o} or @option{-O}.  Diagnostics are written to standard
error, or to @option{--stderr}.  @option{--help} and @option{--version}
are written to standard output and exit successfully.

A transfer error has the form @samp{twist: (@var{n}) @var{message}} and
exits @var{n}, a status from the table below.  A bad command line exits
2, with no number in parentheses, and suggests @option{--help}.
@option{-s} hides a transfer diagnostic.  @option{-S} shows it again.
Several URLs leave the status of the last transfer, unless
@option{--fail-early} stops at the first failure.

@table @option
@item -h
@itemx --help
Print a help message on standard output and exit successfully.
@item --version
Print version and licensing information on standard output and exit
successfully.
@item -o @var{file}
@itemx --output @var{file}
Write the response body to @var{file}.  @code{-} is standard output.
@item -O
@itemx --remote-name
Write the body to the last segment of the URL path.
@item --output-dir @var{dir}
Directory for a relative @option{-o} or @option{-O} name.  Reset by
@option{--next}.
@item --create-dirs
Create missing parent directories for @option{-o} and @option{-O}.
Reset by @option{--next}.
@item -X @var{method}
@itemx --request @var{method}
Request method.
@item -H @var{line}
@itemx --header @var{line}
Extra request header.  Repeat for each header.
@item -d @var{data}
@itemx --data @var{data}
Request body.  A leading @code{@@} reads a file, and @code{@@-} reads
standard input.  Carriage returns and line feeds are removed.
Repeated @option{-d} values are joined with @code{&}.
@item --data-binary @var{data}
Request body, kept as given, including a file read with @code{@@}.
@item --data-raw @var{data}
Request body.  @code{@@} is not a file.
@item --data-urlencode @var{data}
Form field.  The value is percent-encoded.  @code{name=value} encodes
the value, @code{name@@file} encodes the file, and @code{@@file} encodes
the file with no name.
@item -G
@itemx --get
Append @option{-d} to the query string instead of sending a request
body.
@item -T @var{file}
@itemx --upload-file @var{file}
Upload @var{file} with PUT.  @code{-} reads standard input.  For
@code{sftp} and @code{scp} the file is sent as it is read, so a large file or
a pipe is not held in memory.  For other schemes it is read before the
transfer starts.
@item -I
@itemx --head
Fetch headers only.
@item -i
@itemx --include
Write response headers before the body.
@item -D @var{file}
@itemx --dump-header @var{file}
Write response headers to @var{file}.  @code{-} is standard output.
@item -L
@itemx --location
Follow redirections.
@item --max-redirs @var{n}
Redirect limit.  The default is 50.
@item -f
@itemx --fail
Fail the transfer when the HTTP status is 400 or above, and do not
write the body.  Without this option a 404 is a successful transfer:
the status is recorded and the body is written.
@item --fail-early
Stop at the first failed URL.  This applies to every URL on the
command line.
@item --compressed
Ask for a gzip or deflate response and decompress it.
@item --content-encoding @var{coding}
Compress the request body.  @var{coding} is @code{gzip} or
@code{deflate}.  The request carries @code{Content-Encoding}.  Reset
by @option{--next}.
@item -k
@itemx --insecure
Do not check the certificate of the server for TLS, including HTTP/3: any
chain is accepted, whoever it names.  A file or directory of authorities that
the environment names and that cannot be read does not matter then.  For
@code{sftp} and @code{scp} it skips the
@file{known_hosts} check (@pxref{SFTP and SCP}); a fingerprint given with
@option{--hostpubsha256} is still checked.
@item -x @var{url}
@itemx --proxy @var{url}
Proxy.  Use @code{http://}, @code{https://} or @code{socks5://}
(@code{socks5h://} is the same), with a port (the default is 8080 for HTTP,
443 for HTTPS and 1080 for SOCKS) and, if the proxy wants them,
@samp{user:password@@} before the host, percent-encoded.  An IPv6 proxy is in
brackets.  @code{socks4://} proxies are refused.  For @code{sftp} and
@code{scp} (@pxref{SFTP and SCP}) the proxy opens a tunnel.  @option{-x ""}
uses no proxy, whatever the environment says.  A proxy is also taken from the
environment (@pxref{Environment}).

An @code{https://} proxy is spoken to in TLS, and its certificate is checked.
The proxy is asked, in that TLS, to @code{CONNECT} to the target: in a stream
of HTTP/2 if it speaks that, and on the connection if it speaks only
HTTP/1.1, as ALPN says.  What the target's protocol sends, including its own
TLS, goes through that tunnel, so the proxy sees where the transfer goes and
how much, but not what.  Credentials go to it as Basic, in the TLS.  This works
for every scheme that can use a proxy except @code{ftp} and @code{ftps}, and an
@code{http://} URL is tunnelled too.  A tunnel has a connection to the proxy of
its own.

HTTP/3 (@option{--http3-only}) goes through an @code{https://} proxy that
speaks HTTP/3 and does @code{CONNECT-UDP} (RFC 9298): QUIC to the proxy, a
@code{CONNECT} on a stream of it that asks for a UDP flow to the target, and the
target's QUIC packets in HTTP Datagrams (RFC 9297) on that stream.  The target's
TLS is still its own.  The proxy looks the target up, so its name is not looked
up here.  A proxy of another kind cannot carry HTTP/3, and then the transfer
fails, and does not go round the proxy.  The proxy's address is looked up, and
its certificate checked, as for TCP.  A failure of the proxy's, such as a 403 or
a 407, is exit status 56.
@item --proxy-template-udp @var{template}
The URI Template (RFC 6570) of a proxy that does @code{CONNECT-UDP}: an
@code{https://} URI with the variables @code{target_host} and
@code{target_port}, as in
@samp{https://proxy.example/masque/@{target_host@}/@{target_port@}/} or
@samp{https://proxy.example/masque@{?target_host,target_port@}}.  Without it
the template of @option{-x https://host:port} is
@samp{https://host:port/.well-known/masque/udp/@{target_host@}/@{target_port@}/}.
With it and no @option{-x}, the template names the proxy for UDP (HTTP/3), and
every other transfer is direct.  An invalid template is a usage error (exit
status 2).  Credentials come from the proxy's URL, the template's authority, or
@option{-U}.
@item --proxy-cacert @var{file}
PEM bundle of the authorities that vouch for an https proxy.  Without it the
proxy is checked against the same authorities as the target:
@option{--cacert}, @option{--capath}, the environment's (@pxref{Environment}),
and the system's.
@item --proxy-insecure
Do not check the certificate of an https proxy.  This is not @option{-k}: that
says nothing about the proxy, and the proxy is checked unless this is given.
@item --noproxy @var{list}
Comma-separated hosts that do not go through a proxy, also when one is
given with @option{-x}.  A name matches itself and the names under it:
@samp{local.com} matches @samp{local.com} and @samp{www.local.com}, and
not @samp{www.notlocal.com}.  An address matches itself, or a network as
@samp{192.168.0.0/16}.  @samp{*} is every host, which is to use no proxy.
This overrides the environment's @env{no_proxy} and @env{NO_PROXY}, and
@option{--noproxy ""} turns them off.
@item -U @var{user:password}
@itemx --proxy-user @var{user:password}
The user name and password for the proxy, if its URL has none.
@item -A @var{name}
@itemx --user-agent @var{name}
User-Agent header.
@item -u @var{user[:password]}
@itemx --user @var{user[:password]}
User and password.  Without a colon, the password is read from
@file{/dev/tty} with echo disabled, so a piped standard input is left
for @option{-d} or @option{-T}.
@item -e @var{url}
@itemx --referer @var{url}
Referer header.
@item -r @var{range}
@itemx --range @var{range}
Byte range.  For @code{sftp} and @code{scp} it is @samp{@var{first}-@var{last}},
@samp{@var{first}-}, or @samp{-@var{count}} for the last @var{count} bytes
(@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).
@item -l
@itemx --list-only
List the names only, one to a line, not the server's long form, when the URL
names a directory.  For @code{sftp} (@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).
@item -Q @var{command}
@itemx --quote @var{command}
Run an SFTP command on the server before the transfer, or after a successful
one if @var{command} begins with @samp{-}.  A @samp{*} before it lets the
server refuse it without ending the transfer.  May be given many times.  For
@code{sftp} URLs only; other URLs ignore it (@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).
@item --limit-rate @var{speed}
Keep to @var{speed} bytes a second, for downloads and uploads, for
@code{sftp} and @code{scp}.  The speed is a whole number with an optional
suffix: @samp{k}, @samp{m}, @samp{g}, @samp{t} or @samp{p}, in either case,
each 1024 times the one before (@samp{200K}, @samp{3m}).  @samp{0} is no
limit.  Other kinds of URL do not keep to a rate yet, and are refused with
status 2 rather than ignoring the limit.
@item --ftp-create-dirs
Make the directories an @code{sftp} upload needs, with mode 0755.
@item -C @var{offset}
@itemx --continue-at @var{offset}
Resume at byte @var{offset}, for @code{sftp} and @code{scp}.  Use @samp{-} to
find the offset.  A download continues from the end of the file named by
@option{-o} or @option{-O}, which is opened to add to it; with a number the
file is added to from that byte of the remote file.  An upload with
@option{-T} skips that many bytes of the local file and adds to the remote
one at that offset; with @samp{-} the server is asked how big the remote file
is.  Exits 36 if the remote file is shorter than the offset.
@item -b @var{data}
@itemx --cookie @var{data}
Cookie request header.  This is not a cookie jar file.
@item --oauth2-bearer @var{token}
Bearer token.
@item -m @var{seconds}
@itemx --max-time @var{seconds}
Limit for the whole transfer.  Up to three decimal places are
accepted.
@item --connect-timeout @var{seconds}
Limit for making the connection, in seconds; five minutes if it is not given
(or is 0).  For @code{sftp} and @code{scp} it covers all of it: the name
lookup, the TCP connect, a proxy, and the SSH handshake up to the login
(@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).  Up to three decimal places are accepted.
@item -s
@itemx --silent
Hide the progress meter and the transfer diagnostic.  The exit status
is unchanged.  @option{--no-silent} undoes it.
@item -S
@itemx --show-error
Show the transfer diagnostic when @option{-s} was also given, in either
order.  @option{--no-show-error} undoes it.
@item -#
@itemx --progress-bar
Show the progress as a bar in place of the meter (@pxref{Progress
meter}).  @option{--no-progress-bar} undoes it.
@item --no-progress-meter
Hide the progress meter and the bar, and leave the diagnostics alone.
@option{--progress-meter} undoes it.
@item --stderr @var{file}
Write diagnostics to @var{file}.  @code{-} keeps standard error.  This
applies to every URL.
@item -w @var{format}
@itemx --write-out @var{format}
Print @var{format} on standard output after the body.
@code{@{http_code@}}, @code{@{response_code@}},
@code{@{size_download@}}, @code{@{url_effective@}},
@code{@{content_type@}}, @code{@{exitcode@}}, and @code{@{errormsg@}}
are replaced.  @code{\n}, @code{\r}, @code{\t}, and @code{%%} are
recognised.  An unknown @code{@{name@}} is empty.
@item --url @var{url}
A URL to fetch.  It is the same as a URL given without an option, and can be
given as an expansion (@option{--expand-url}).
@item --variable @var{name=text}
@itemx --variable @var{name@@file}
@itemx --variable %@var{name}
Set a variable (@pxref{Variables}).  @samp{name@@file} reads it from a file,
or from standard input for @samp{-}; @samp{%name} takes it from the
environment, and @samp{%name=text} or @samp{%name@@file} says what it is if
the environment does not have it.
@item --expand-@var{option} @var{parameter}
Any option that takes a parameter, with the variables in the parameter
expanded first (@pxref{Variables}): @option{--expand-url},
@option{--expand-data}, @option{--expand-header}, @option{--expand-output}.
@item -g
@itemx --globoff
Do not expand @samp{@{@}} and @samp{[]} in URLs (@pxref{URL globbing}).
@item -q
@itemx --disable
Accepted.  No config file is read.
@item --next
Start a separate transfer for the following URL.  Options after it do
not inherit the previous URL's method, body, headers, output, proxy,
user, or timeouts.  @option{-s}, @option{-S}, @option{-#},
@option{--no-progress-meter}, @option{--stderr}, and
@option{--fail-early} still apply.  A URL is required before
@option{--next}.
@item -E @var{file}
@itemx --cert @var{file}
Client certificate in PEM form, leaf first.  Further certificates in
the file are sent after the leaf.  The matching private key is
@option{--key}, an unencrypted PEM file.  When a server asks for a
client certificate and none was given, the handshake fails.  When one
was given, it is presented.
@item --key @var{file}
Private key for @option{--cert}.  For @code{sftp} and @code{scp} it is the
private key to log in with, and it is used alone: an ssh-agent is not asked
(@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).
@item --pass @var{phrase}
Passphrase for the private key file, for the formats twist can decrypt
(@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).
@item --hostpubsha256 @var{hash}
The SHA-256 of the host key of an @code{sftp} or @code{scp} server, in base64
as @command{ssh-keygen -l} prints it, with or without @samp{SHA256:}.  A host
key that matches is trusted, whatever @file{known_hosts} says; one that does
not is refused, even with @option{-k}.
@item --cacert @var{file}
PEM trust bundle used to verify the peer.
@item --capath @var{directory}
A directory of certificate authorities, as PEM files whose names end in
@file{.pem}, @file{.crt} or @file{.cer}, or are the eight-digit hash and
number that OpenSSL's @command{c_rehash} makes.  It is used as well as
@option{--cacert}.  With neither, the environment's (@pxref{Environment}),
and then the system's authorities are trusted.
@item --http1.0
@itemx --http1.1
@itemx --http2
@itemx --http2-prior-knowledge
@itemx --http3
@itemx --http3-only
Select the HTTP version.  HTTP/3 is used when discovered from an HTTPS
record or a cached Alt-Svc entry.  HTTP/2 on HTTPS is ALPN.  Cleartext
HTTP/2 is an h2c upgrade, unless prior knowledge was requested.
@item --dns-order @var{list}
Comma-separated DNS transports.  The default is @code{doq,dot,doh,udp}.
DoQ and DoT are used when the system resolver advertises them, or when
@option{--doq-url} or @option{--dot-url} names a server.  When one of
@option{--doq-url}, @option{--dot-url} or @option{--doh-url} names a
server and this option is not given, only the named servers are asked,
in that order, and plain UDP is not: a server that is down, or that has
been made unreachable, ends the lookup and does not send the name to the
system resolver in the clear.  List @code{udp} to allow it.  UDP retries
over TCP when the answer is truncated.  A host that is an IPv4 or IPv6
address is used as that address.
@item --doh-url @var{url}
Resolve host names through this DNS-over-HTTPS server instead of
@option{--dns-order}.  The URL must use HTTPS.  The server's name is
resolved with DoQ, DoT, or UDP, and not with DoH.  Each query is then an
HTTP POST whose body is one DNS message
(@code{application/dns-message}).  An empty URL
restores the normal resolver.  When @option{--dns-order} is also set,
that list is used and this URL supplies the @code{doh} step.
@item --doh-insecure
Do not verify the DoH server certificate.  This is independent of
@option{-k}.
@item --doq-url @var{url}
DNS-over-QUIC server.  @code{quic://host} or @code{host}, with an
optional port (default 853).  The certificate is checked against that
host.  The host name is resolved with UDP.  An empty URL clears it.
Without this option, DoQ uses the address and name from a
@code{_dns.resolver.arpa} SVCB record (DDR).
@item --dot-url @var{url}
DNS-over-TLS server.  @code{tls://host} or @code{host}, with an optional
port (default 853).  Same discovery rules as @option{--doq-url}.
@item --mail-from @var{address}
@itemx --mail-rcpt @var{address}
SMTP envelope.  Repeat @option{--mail-rcpt} for each recipient.
@end table

@code{mqtt://host/topic} subscribes to the topic, or publishes
@option{--data} and disconnects.  @code{ldap://host/dn} binds, searches,
and writes the result as LDIF.  @code{ws://host/path} does the HTTP/1
upgrade, or an extended CONNECT on HTTP/2 or HTTP/3, and writes payload
bytes.  An HTTP/1 status other than 101 fails.  @code{gopher://host/type}
sends the selector and writes the reply until the server closes.
@code{dict://host/d:word:database} defines a word,
@code{dict://host/m:word:database:strategy} matches one, and
@code{dict://host/show:db} asks the server for its database list.  The
reply is written until the server closes.
@code{tftp://host/file} reads a file.  @option{--data} or @option{-T}
uploads one.  The transfer offers block size 512 and retransmits a
lost packet.  A proxy is not used.  A server error packet exits 68
through 74.

There is no config file, @option{--fail-with-body}, verbose trace,
cookie jar, or parallel transfer.

@heading Exit status
The numbers are curl's, so that a script that tests them works with either.

@table @asis
@item 0
The transfer finished.
@item 1
The scheme is not implemented.
@item 2
The command line is wrong, or twist could not initialise.
@item 3
The URL is malformed.
@item 6
The host name could not be resolved.
@item 7
The connection failed.
@item 8
The server's reply could not be used.
@item 18
The transfer ended before all of the data the server announced had come.
@item 21
A @option{--quote} command of an @code{sftp} transfer failed.
@item 22
@option{--fail} and the HTTP status is 400 or above.
@item 23
The output could not be written.
@item 26
A local file named by @option{-d}, @option{--data-binary},
@option{--data-urlencode}, or @option{-T} could not be read.
@item 27
Out of memory.
@item 25
An @code{sftp} or @code{scp} upload failed.
@item 28
The transfer timed out.
@item 33
The range cannot be delivered: it starts beyond the end of the remote file, or
is not one twist understands.
@item 36
The transfer cannot be resumed: the remote file is shorter than the offset, the
local data is, or the file to resume is not there.
@item 35
The TLS handshake failed.
@item 37
A file URL could not be opened.
@item 42
The transfer was aborted.
@item 43
A bad argument was passed to the library.
@item 47
Too many redirects.
@item 52
The server closed before sending any data.
@item 56
A proxy refused a @code{CONNECT} or @code{CONNECT-UDP} tunnel, with a
status such as 403 or 407.
@item 61
The content encoding could not be decoded.
@item 9
The SFTP server would not let the user read the file.
@item 60
The host key of an @code{sftp} or @code{scp} server is not trusted: it is
not in @file{known_hosts}, it differs from the one there, or it does not
match @option{--hostpubsha256}.
@item 67
Authentication failed.
@item 68
TFTP file not found.
@item 69
TFTP access violation.
@item 70
TFTP disk full.
@item 71
TFTP illegal operation.
@item 72
TFTP unknown transfer.
@item 73
TFTP file already exists.
@item 74
TFTP no such user.
@item 78
The file does not exist on the @code{sftp} or @code{scp} server.
@item 79
The @code{sftp} or @code{scp} server could not do what was asked.
@item 81
The operation could not be started.
@item 93
A function of the multi interface was called from inside a callback.  Only
a program that uses the library can cause this.
@item 97
A SOCKS5 proxy refused the connection or could not complete it.
@end table

@node Protocols
@chapter Protocols

A URL scheme selects the protocol.  The scheme with an @samp{s} is the
same protocol inside TLS from the first byte; no scheme is upgraded to
TLS after it starts.

@table @code
@item dict
Looks up a word with a DICT server: DEFINE, MATCH or SHOW, chosen by
the URL path.  The reply is written until the server closes.
@item file
Reads the local file named by the URL path.
@item ftp, ftps
Downloads a file, or lists a directory when the URL path ends in a
slash.  @code{ftps} is TLS from the first byte (port 990); @code{ftp}
is never upgraded.  On @code{ftps} the data connection is protected as
well (RFC 4217): after login twist sends @code{PBSZ 0} and @code{PROT P},
runs TLS on each data connection, and checks the server against the host
of the URL as it does the control connection (@option{-k} turns both
off).  The data connection resumes the TLS session of the control
connection when the server gave a ticket for it, since servers commonly
insist on that.  A server that will not protect the data connection ends
the transfer with status 35; the file is never fetched unprotected
instead.
@item gopher, gophers
Sends the selector from the URL and writes the reply until the server
closes.
@item http, https
HTTP/1.0, HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, as the @option{--http*} options
and the server allow.
@item imap, imaps
Fetches a message from a mailbox, or lists the mailboxes.
@item ldap, ldaps
Binds and searches, and writes the result as LDIF.
@item mqtt, mqtts
Subscribes to the topic in the URL path, or publishes @option{--data}
and disconnects.
@item pop3, pop3s
Retrieves a message, or lists the messages in the maildrop.
@item scp, sftp
Both run over SFTP on an SSH connection: a file up or down and, for
@code{sftp}, a directory listing.  There is no SCP wire protocol.  SSH
provides the encryption, so there is no TLS variant.  See @ref{SFTP and
SCP}.
@item smtp, smtps
Sends a message to the server, from @option{--mail-from} to each
@option{--mail-rcpt}.
@item tftp
Reads a file, or uploads one with @option{--data} or @option{-T}.  It is
a datagram protocol, so no proxy is used.
@item ws, wss
WebSocket, over HTTP/1, HTTP/2 or HTTP/3.  The payload bytes are
written.
@end table

The schemes with a trailing @code{s} (@code{imaps}, @code{ldaps},
@code{mqtts}, @code{pop3s}, @code{smtps}, @code{https}, @code{ftps},
@code{wss}) are TLS from the first byte.  The ones without are not upgraded:
twist has no @code{STARTTLS}, @code{STLS} or @code{AUTH TLS}, and no option
to require them.  A user name and password given for @code{smtp},
@code{pop3}, @code{imap}, @code{ldap}, @code{mqtt} or @code{ftp} are sent on
a connection that is not encrypted, and by a method such as @code{PLAIN},
@code{LOGIN} or a simple bind that does not hide them; @code{PLAIN} is tried
when the server advertises none.  Use the scheme with the @code{s}.

These schemes are recognised, but are not implemented, and exit 1:
@code{rtmp}, @code{rtmps}, @code{rtsp}, @code{smb}, @code{smbs} and
@code{telnet}.

@node Progress meter
@chapter Progress meter

Twist shows the progress of a transfer on standard error, or on
@option{--stderr}: a heading of two lines and then one
line that is written over and over, with the amount transferred, the
speeds, and the time spent and left:

@example
  % Total    % Received % Xferd  Average Speed   Time    Time     Time  Current
                                 Dload  Upload   Total   Spent    Left  Speed
100 80000  100 80000    0     0  40000      0  0:00:02  0:00:02 --:--:-- 40000
@end example

The sizes are in bytes, and with the suffixes k, M, G, T and P as
multiples of 1024.  The columns are the percentage and size of the whole
transfer, the percentage and amount received, the percentage and amount
sent, the average download and upload speeds, the time the transfer will
take, the time it has taken and the time it has left, and the speed
over the last few seconds.  Where a size is not known, the percentages
are 0 and the times @samp{--:--:--}.  The line is drawn when the second
changes, after a second with nothing to draw, and once more at the end.
The end of the line is a newline.

The meter is not shown when the body is written to a terminal, since
it would mix with it: a download, or an upload to HTTP, with no
@option{-o} or @option{-O}, on a terminal.  It is not shown with
@option{-s} or @option{--no-progress-meter}.  @option{-#} draws a bar
instead: a line of @samp{#} signs across the screen and the percentage
when the size is known, and when it is not a spaceship with hash signs
flying above it.  The width is that of the terminal, or the environment
variable @env{COLUMNS} if that is a number from 21 to 9999, and 79 if
neither is known.  The bar ends its line after any error message.

A size is known for the body of an HTTP response that has
Content-Length, a file, an FTP download whose server says how big it is,
an sftp or scp download, an IMAP message, and the data sent with
@option{-d}, @option{-T}, or an SMTP, MQTT, TFTP or sftp upload.  A
redirect finishes the line, and the next response starts again at 0.

@option{-s}, @option{-S}, @option{-#}, @option{--no-progress-meter} and
@option{--stderr} apply to every URL, also after @option{--next}.

A program that uses the library gets the same from
@code{TWISTOPT_NOPROGRESS} (1 by default, which shows nothing),
@code{TWISTOPT_STDERR}, and a callback of its own,
@code{TWISTOPT_XFERINFOFUNCTION}, which is given the totals and counts
about once a second and when anything changes.  The callback returns 0
to draw nothing, @code{TWIST_PROGRESSFUNC_CONTINUE} to have the library
draw its meter as well, and anything else to stop the transfer with
@code{TWIST_ERR_ABORTED}.  @code{TWISTOPT_INFILESIZE} says how much an
upload with a read function will give.

@node Environment
@chapter Environment

The variables can be in lower case or upper case, and the lower case one
has precedence.  @env{http_proxy} is the exception: it is only read in lower
case, since a web server that runs a program sets the upper case one from the
request.

A proxy from the environment has the same effect as @option{-x}, which
overrides it.  Its value is a proxy URL as @option{-x} takes, with
@code{http://}, @code{https://} or @code{socks5://} before the host, and
@samp{user:password@@} if it wants them.  An empty value is no proxy.

@table @env
@item http_proxy [protocol://]host[:port]
The proxy for HTTP.
@item HTTPS_PROXY [protocol://]host[:port]
The proxy for HTTPS.
@item [url-protocol]_PROXY [protocol://]host[:port]
The proxy for the scheme of the URL, whatever it is: @env{FTP_PROXY},
@env{FTPS_PROXY}, @env{SFTP_PROXY}, @env{IMAP_PROXY}, @env{SMTP_PROXY},
@env{LDAP_PROXY} and so on, for the schemes that can go through a proxy.
@env{WS_PROXY} and @env{WSS_PROXY} are those of WebSocket; if they are not set
the proxy of HTTP and HTTPS is used.
@item ALL_PROXY [protocol://]host[:port]
The proxy for a scheme that has none of its own.
@item NO_PROXY comma-separated list of hosts and domains
Hosts that do not go through a proxy.  The list is as for
@option{--noproxy}, which overrides it: a name matches itself and the names
under it, an address matches itself or a network written as
@samp{192.168.0.0/16}, an IPv6 address is written without brackets, and a
single @samp{*} matches all hosts.  This disables the proxy also when it was
given with @option{-x}.
@item COLUMNS terminal width
If it is a number from 21 to 9999, that many characters are the width of the
bar of @option{-#}.  Without it the width of the terminal is used, and 79 if
there is none.
@item HOME directory
Where @file{.ssh/known_hosts} is looked for, for sftp and scp.  If it is not
set, the home directory in the password file.
@item QLOGDIR directory name
Makes twist write a log of each QUIC connection (HTTP/3 and DNS over QUIC) in
that directory, in a file named after the connection ID the client chose, in
hexadecimal, and the ending @file{.qlog}.  Each line is an event of the
connection, as draft-ietf-quic-qlog-quic-events defines them, with the time in
microseconds, its name and its fields as @samp{KEY=VALUE}.  The file can be read
by the owner only.  A directory that is not there means no log.  These files
can become large, and say who was talked to.
@item SSH_AUTH_SOCK file name
The socket of the ssh-agent that holds the keys for sftp and scp, as for ssh.
@item SSL_CERT_DIR directory
If set, it is used as the @option{--capath} value.
@item SSL_CERT_FILE file name
If set, it is used as the @option{--cacert} value.
@item SSLKEYLOGFILE file name
If you set this to a file name, twist writes the secrets of its TLS
connections in that file, in the NSS key log format, so that Wireshark and
similar tools can decrypt a capture of them.  This includes HTTP/3.  The file
is made so that only its owner can read it, and what is in it undoes the
encryption of the connections: it is for debugging.
@item TWIST_CA_BUNDLE file name
If set, it is used as the @option{--cacert} value, and @env{SSL_CERT_DIR} and
@env{SSL_CERT_FILE} are not looked at.
@end table

The names of the certificate variables are looked at only when neither
@option{--cacert} nor @option{--capath} is given.  A file or directory that
they name and that cannot be read is an error.

Twist reads no configuration file.  Variables can also be imported by @option{--variable %@var{name}} (@pxref{Variables}).

@node Variables
@chapter Variables

A command line can set variables and use them in the parameters of options.

@example
twist --variable name=smith --expand-url 'https://example.com/@{@{name@}@}' ...
twist --variable who=smith --expand-data '@{"user": "@{@{who:json@}@}"@}' ...
twist --variable '%HOME' --expand-output '@{@{HOME@}@}/page.html' URL
@end example

@option{--variable name=text} gives @var{name} the text; @option{--variable
name@@file} gives it the contents of the file, and @samp{-} for the file is
standard input.  A name is letters, digits and underscores, and case matters;
the content can be anything, to ten megabytes.  A variable that is set again
has its new content.  The environment is imported by name: @option{--variable
%name} takes it from there and is an error if the environment does not have it;
@option{--variable %name=text} (or @samp{%name@@file}) says what it is when the
environment does not, and the environment's value is used when it does, even if
that is empty.

A variable is used in the parameter of an option whose name has @samp{expand-}
put before it.  @option{--expand-data} is @option{--data} with each
@samp{@{@{name@}@}} in the parameter replaced by the content.  The option
may be given as @option{--expand-url=value} too.  This works for every long
option that takes a parameter, and not for a URL given as an argument, which
is not an option: use @option{--url} for that.  A variable is what it is at the
place on the command line where it is used, so it is set before it is needed;
variables stay across @option{--next}.

@itemize
@item
A name that is not set is empty.
@item
@samp{\@{@{} is @samp{@{@{} and is not the start of a variable.
@item
Anything that is not @samp{@{@{name@}@}} (with a name of the right kind, and no
spaces) is left as it is.
@item
The content is not expanded again.
@item
The expanded text is a string, so content with a NUL in it is an error unless
a function encodes it (@code{b64}, @code{json} or @code{url}).
@item
The result may not be over ten megabytes.
@end itemize

Functions follow the name after colons, and are applied from left to right:
@samp{@{@{name:trim:b64@}@}}.  A comma can separate them too.

@table @code
@item trim
Takes the white space off both ends.
@item json
Writes the content as the inside of a JSON string: quotes, backslashes and
control characters escaped, UTF-8 left as it is.  The quotes are not added.
@item url
Percent-encodes everything but letters, digits and @samp{-._~}.
@item b64
Base64.
@item 64dec
From base64, padded or not.  What is not base64 gives @samp{[64dec-fail]}.
@end table

An error in a variable or an expansion is a bad option: the message names the
option and what is wrong, and the status is 2 (26 for a file that cannot be
read).  A URL that comes out of an expansion is globbed afterwards
(@pxref{URL globbing}), so content with braces or brackets in it, put in a URL,
should go through @code{url} or be used with @option{-g}.

@node URL globbing
@chapter URL globbing

A URL may name many things at once, for every scheme:

@table @code
@item @{a,b,c@}
One of these, in turn.  A set cannot hold another set or a range, and an
empty element is the empty string.  In a set a backslash makes the character
after it plain.
@item [1-10]
A range of numbers.  @samp{[01-10]} keeps the width of its start, with
zeros, and @samp{[1-10:3]} takes every third.
@item [a-z]
A range of letters of one case, which also takes a step.
@end table

A URL with several makes all the combinations, in order, the last going
fastest: @samp{@{x,y@}/[1-2]} is @samp{x/1}, @samp{x/2}, @samp{y/1},
@samp{y/2}.  They are made one at a time, so a range of billions starts at
once.  Outside a set a backslash before a brace or bracket makes it plain, and
the brackets of an IPv6 address are not a range.  A pattern that is wrong is a
malformed URL (status 3) and the message shows the place:

@example
twist: (3) bad range in URL position 25:
http://127.0.0.1:1/[3-1]
                        ^
@end example

The name given to @option{-o} may hold @samp{#1}, @samp{#2} and so on, for
what the first glob, the second, and so on, were in the URL that is being
fetched: @samp{twist -o 'page_#1.html' 'https://example.com/p[1-3].html'}
writes @file{page_1.html} to @file{page_3.html}.  A number with no glob is
text, as is a @samp{#} with no number; a backslash does not stop it.  The
@option{-o} goes with the URL it is given next to, and so with everything that
URL makes: a name with no @samp{#} is written by each in turn.  @option{-O}
names each file from its own URL.  Each transfer is a transfer: a failure of
one does not stop the rest unless @option{--fail-early} says so, and the
status is the last one's.  @option{-g} turns it all off.

Over @code{sftp} the URLs for one server share a connection, so
@samp{sftp://host/dir/f[1-100].txt} logs in once.  There is no wildcard
in a remote path, such as @samp{*.txt}.

@node SFTP and SCP
@chapter SFTP and SCP

@example
twist sftp://user@@host/path/to/file
twist scp://user@@host/~/file
@end example

twist transfers a file over SSH for each URL, up or down, and lists a directory
for an @code{sftp} URL.  Several URLs on a command line are several transfers,
and those for one server share a connection (see @emph{Several URLs} below).  URLs are
expanded as any URL is (@pxref{URL globbing}) with @samp{@{a,b@}} and
@samp{[1-3]}, but a path has no wildcard such as @samp{*}.  @code{scp://} and @code{sftp://} are the same transfer, which uses the SFTP subsystem of the server: twist does not
implement the old SCP protocol, as OpenSSH's own @command{scp} has not since
version 9.0.  A server with no SFTP subsystem fails with an error that says
so.  The path is absolute, except that @samp{/~/} begins in the directory
the server puts the user in.  An @code{scp} URL does not expand wildcards.

@subheading Directory listings

An @code{sftp} URL that ends in @samp{/}, or has no path, lists that
directory: @samp{sftp://host/} the root, @samp{sftp://host/~/} the user's home
directory, @samp{sftp://host/dir/} a directory.  Each entry is a line, in
the long form the server gives (like @command{ls -l}), in the order the server
sends them, including @file{.}, @file{..} and files whose names begin with a
dot.  @option{-l} gives the names only.

What a server sends is not trusted with the output: a newline in a file name
would make a line that is not an entry, and control characters can do as they
please to a terminal.  So control characters in a listing, and the UTF-8 form
of the C1 ones, are replaced with @samp{?}. 

An @code{scp} URL does not list: it names one file, and a URL that ends in
@samp{/} exits 78.  A directory that is not there, or a file followed by a
slash, exits 78 too, and one that may not be listed exits 9.  A range or
@option{-C} means nothing for a listing and is refused.

@subheading Upload, resume and ranges

@option{-T @var{file}} uploads a file.  The remote file is created, with mode
0644, and truncated if it is there.  When the URL names a
directory (it ends in @samp{/}) the file gets the name of the local file.
Data given with @option{-d} is uploaded the same way.  If the
file cannot be read part way, the transfer fails with status 26; the remote
file is left as far as it got.  A pipe is read as it delivers
data, and twist does not do anything else while it waits for the next of it.

@option{-C} continues a transfer that stopped.  An upload does not send what
the server has, and writes on at that offset of the remote file without
truncating it.  A download is added to the end of the output file.

@option{-r} downloads part of a file: @samp{100-199} (both ends included),
@samp{100-} (to the end), or @samp{-50} (the last 50 bytes).  A range that
starts beyond the end of the file exits 33.  A range and @option{-C} cannot
be used together.

The size of the remote file is asked for only when it is needed: to check a
download resume, to find the start of @samp{-50}, or to resume an upload.
A server that does not say how big a file is cannot do the last, nor
@samp{-50}.

@subheading Time limits

@option{--connect-timeout} is one limit for all of making the connection: the
name lookup, the TCP connect, a proxy's tunnel, the SSH banner, the key
exchange and the login.  A server or a proxy that takes the connection and does
not go on is given up on with status 28, where it would otherwise be waited for.
It ends at the login: a transfer that takes longer than the limit is not cut
off by it, and a session that is kept for the next URL is not connecting at all.
The key exchange gets the same time, not a limit of its own.
@option{--max-time} limits the whole, as before.

@subheading Through a proxy

@option{-x} opens a tunnel to the SSH server through an HTTP proxy
(@code{CONNECT}) or a SOCKS5 proxy, and the SSH session goes through it.
Credentials in the proxy's URL, or given with @option{-U}, are sent as Basic
for HTTP and as a user name and password for SOCKS5.  A refusal is told
with a status: 56 for a @code{CONNECT} that failed
(@samp{CONNECT tunnel failed, response 403}), 97 for SOCKS5 (@samp{Can't
complete SOCKS5 connection to host. (5)}); a proxy that is not there is 7.
The server's banner may arrive in the same packet as the proxy's reply, and
a proxy may say a good deal before it opens the tunnel; both are handled.

The server is named to the proxy as the URL gives it: a name is sent as a name,
and an IP address as an address (an IPv6 one in brackets for @code{CONNECT}).
@code{socks5://} and @code{socks5h://} are the same: twist does not look the
name up itself before giving it to a SOCKS5 proxy.  The tunnel is kept for the next URL as a connection is, but
only for the same proxy with the same credentials: a login made through one
proxy is not used by a transfer through another, or through none.

@subheading IPv6 addresses

An IPv6 address is written in brackets, @samp{sftp://user@@[2001:db8::7]:2222/f}.
known_hosts names it as @command{ssh} does: bare for port 22
(@samp{2001:db8::7}), and in brackets with the port for any other
(@samp{[2001:db8::7]:2222}).  A link-local address names the interface it is
reached by after a percent sign, which a URL writes encoded:
@samp{[fe80::1%25eth0]} (a bare @samp{%eth0} is taken too).  known_hosts keeps
the zone, @samp{fe80::1%eth0}, as @command{ssh} does, and the connection goes
out by that interface; without it a link-local address goes nowhere.  An
interface may be given by number as well.  What is between brackets must be
an IPv6 address: anything else is a malformed URL (status 3), and an interface
that this machine does not have is a connection that cannot be made (status
7).  Neither waits for a lookup.  The effective URL (@samp{%@{url_effective@}})
keeps the brackets and the zone, and an HTTP request names the host in its
@code{Host} header with the brackets and without the zone.

@subheading Commands and missing directories

@option{-Q @var{command}} runs a command on the server before the transfer,
and @option{-Q -@var{command}} after it, if it succeeded.  An @code{sftp} URL
takes them; an @code{scp} URL, like any other kind, ignores them.  They run
one at a time, in the order given, and the first the server refuses ends the
transfer with status 21 and a message that says which command and why.  A
@samp{*} before a command (@samp{-Q '*rm old'}) lets the server refuse it.  A
command twist cannot read is an error even then.  A @samp{+} before a command
is refused: it is the form for FTP.

The command is the word up to
the first space, in any case; a name may be in double quotes to hold spaces,
with @samp{\"} and @samp{\\} for a quote and a backslash in it.  Single quotes
mean nothing.  A relative name is the server's to place, in the user's
directory.

@table @code
@item chmod @var{mode} @var{file}
The mode is octal.
@item chown @var{uid} @var{file}
@itemx chgrp @var{gid} @var{file}
A number.  The other of the two is kept as it is.
@item mtime @var{date} @var{file}
@itemx atime @var{date} @var{file}
The date is one parameter, so in quotes: @samp{"Thu, 02 Jan 2020 10:00:00
GMT"}, with or without the day of the week, a zone as @samp{GMT} or
@samp{+0100} (none: local time), or as @code{asctime} writes it.  The other
time is kept as it is.
@item ln @var{source} @var{link}
@itemx symlink @var{source} @var{link}
Make @var{link} a symbolic link to @var{source}.
@item mkdir @var{dir}
@itemx rmdir @var{dir}
@itemx rm @var{file}
@itemx rename @var{from} @var{to}
As the names say.  @code{mkdir} makes a directory with mode 0755.
@item pwd
The server's current directory (its home), written to the header output
(@option{-D}, @option{-i}) as @samp{257 "/home/me" is current directory.}
@end table

@option{-I} runs the commands and looks at the file, and transfers nothing:
status 78 if it is not there.  That is a way to run commands alone.
@option{--ftp-create-dirs} makes the directories of the path of an upload
that are not there, from the top, and tries again; a directory that is there
is left, and if making them does not help the error is the upload's.

@subheading Rate limits

@option{--limit-rate} limits the average over the transfer.
A chunk is sent or asked for when the bytes before it have been earned at the
limit, so the transfer is ahead by at most one chunk.  A chunk is 32 KiB, or
half a second's worth of the limit when that is less, so a slow limit gives
small requests and not lumps; at 1000 bytes a second the requests are of 500
bytes.  The time spent waiting for a slow disk or a slow pipe is time, so
it counts.  The library takes @code{TWISTOPT_MAX_RECV_SPEED_LARGE} and
@code{TWISTOPT_MAX_SEND_SPEED_LARGE} for the two directions.

@subheading Logging in

A user name or password written in the URL is percent-encoded, as it must be
if it holds an @samp{@@}, a @samp{:} or a @samp{/}: @samp{p@@ss} is
@samp{p%40ss}.  twist uses what the encoding says.  This holds for every
scheme.  A name or password that decodes to a carriage return, a line feed or
a NUL is refused as a malformed URL, since it would otherwise reach a command
the protocol is built from.

Keys are tried in this order.  A key named with @option{--key} is used alone,
and nothing else is asked.  Otherwise the keys in an @command{ssh-agent}, in
the agent's order, then @file{~/.ssh/id_ed25519}, @file{id_ecdsa} and
@file{id_rsa}.  Then a password, from @option{-u} or the URL.

A server may ask for the password as a question (keyboard-interactive login,
usual with PAM) and not as a password.  twist answers when the server asks
one question that is not shown as it is typed, and the answer is the
password.  Anything else, such as a verification code or
several questions, is answered with nothing, so the password is not given to
a question that might not be asking for it; the other ways are then tried, and
if they fail too the message says what was asked.  With no password, the
method is not tried.  A server that offers both a password and questions is
asked the question first, so a wrong password can count as two failed
attempts, as it does for @command{ssh}.

A key may have an OpenSSH user certificate, as @command{ssh} finds it: the
file of the key with @file{-cert.pub} added (@file{~/.ssh/id_ed25519-cert.pub},
or @file{key-cert.pub} for @option{--key key}).  The certificate is offered
before the key, which is still offered after it.  A certificate that cannot
be used is not offered, because the server only says that it refused: if the
login then fails the message says that the certificate has expired, is not yet
good, is not for the key, or does not name the user.  Certificates in an agent
need nothing from twist: @command{ssh-add} loads them with the key.  twist
does not check who signed a certificate; the server decides which CAs it
trusts.

The agent is the one named by the environment variable @env{SSH_AUTH_SOCK}.
twist asks it what it holds and to sign; the private keys never reach twist.
This includes keys that are protected by a passphrase, restricted to certain
servers (@command{ssh-add -h}), or kept on a security key.  Whatever is wrong
with the agent, whether there is none, it does not answer, or it refuses, is
not fatal: the other keys and the password are tried, and if they all fail
the message says what the agent did.  A server that counts every key it turns
away as a failed attempt, as @command{sshd} does, allows a limited number of
keys in the agent to be tried, six unless it is set otherwise.  This is the
server's limit, as it is for @command{ssh}.

The key files twist reads are OpenSSH's, PEM and PKCS#8, and PuTTY's version
3.  @strong{A passphrase-protected key file in the OpenSSH format, which is
what @command{ssh-keygen} writes by default, cannot be read}, because its key
derivation is not in libgcrypt and twist does not take it from anywhere else.
Put the key in an agent (@command{ssh-add}), which can use it, or convert it
with @command{ssh-keygen -p -m PKCS8} and give its passphrase with
@option{--pass}.

The library takes the agent's socket from @code{TWISTOPT_SSH_AGENT_SOCKET}; an
empty string means no agent.

@subheading The host key

The host key is checked against @file{~/.ssh/known_hosts}.  Here, and for the key files above, @file{~} is @env{HOME}, or the home
directory in the password file if @env{HOME} is not set or is empty.  A host that is not in the file, or whose key differs from the one in
it, fails the transfer (status 60); there is no way to accept a new host
key by asking.  Add it with @command{ssh-keyscan}, connect once with
@command{ssh}, or give its fingerprint with @option{--hostpubsha256}.
@option{-k} turns the check off.  A host known by one kind of key is asked
for that kind, so it cannot answer with another.

The library takes the same from @code{TWISTOPT_SSH_KNOWNHOSTS},
@code{TWISTOPT_SSH_HOST_PUBLIC_KEY_SHA256}, @code{TWISTOPT_SSLKEY} and
@code{TWISTOPT_KEYPASSWD}.

@subheading Several URLs

URLs for the same server on one command line are fetched on one connection:
the key exchange and the login happen once, and the SFTP channel stays open.
@code{sftp://} and @code{scp://} share it.  A connection is used again only
for the same host, port and user, and only when everything that decides who
you are or whom twist trusts is the same: the password, the key and its
passphrase, the known_hosts file, a pinned fingerprint, the agent, and whether
the host key is checked at all.  A session that was made with @option{-k}
is never used for a transfer that checks the host key.  A transfer that
fails closes its connection, and the next URL logs in again.  A connection
that is idle for two minutes, or that the server closes, is dropped.

With the library, the connections are kept in the multi handle (or the share)
that the transfers run on.  @code{twist_easy_perform} makes a multi handle of
its own for each call, so it keeps nothing for the next one.

@subheading Requirements

SSH comes from the foxtrot library, which needs libgcrypt 1.12.4 or later.

